      "writtenOn": "2026-03-24T17:36:06Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I see, good catch.\n\n`common/mm/page_alloc.c`\n```\n\t\t/*\n\t\t * We most definitely don\u0027t want callers attempting to\n\t\t * allocate greater than order-1 page units with __GFP_NOFAIL.\n\t\t */\n\t\tWARN_ON_ONCE(order \u003e 1);\n\t\t/*\n\t\t * Also we don\u0027t support __GFP_NOFAIL without __GFP_DIRECT_RECLAIM,\n\t\t * otherwise, we may result in lockup.\n\t\t */\n\t\tWARN_ON_ONCE(!can_direct_reclaim);\n```\n\n\u003e I prefer the current writing because it makes the order-0 case clear.\n\nI agree, although with a small (nit) update to check `order \u003e 0` explicitly, similar to other checks in `page_alloc.c` (which, admittedly, also treats `order` as bool sometimes also):\n\n```\nif ((order \u003e 0 \u0026\u0026 !may_direct_reclaim) || (order \u003e max_order_direct_reclaim))\n```\n\nCan you also add a comment here about why `order \u003e 0` is necessary? Some combination of your description + the `page_alloc.c` comment would be good to help people in the future (or just me).\n\n\u003e otherwise we\u0027d cease from ensuring direct-reclaim is always used for order-0 allocations (which we need to avoid failed allocs when system-wide mem pressure is high).",
      "parentUuid": "d3bc5afc_ae07e72b",
